<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacob Roggeveen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1659">1659</a>–<a href="/years/1729">1729</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacob Roggeveen was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> explorer sent by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> to search for the supposed southern continent. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> Day, 5 April 1722, his ships reached a Pacific island he named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a>, the first known European visit to Rapa Nui. He then sailed on through the Tuamotus and Samoa, but his ships were seized at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> by the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jacob Roggeveen was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middelburg">Middelburg</a>, the capital of the province of Zeeland, on 1 February 1659. His father, Arend Roggeveen, was a mathematician learned in astronomy, geography and the theory of navigation, who studied the supposed southern continent Terra Australis and obtained a patent for an exploring voyage that he never made. Jacob became a notary of Middelburg and took a doctorate in law at the University of Harderwijk on 12 August 1690.</p><p>In 1706 he joined the Dutch East Indies Company and worked from 1707 to 1714 as a councillor of justice at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>. He married twice and was widowed twice, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middelburg">Middelburg</a> alone in 1714. There he became involved in religious controversy by publishing a leaflet of the liberal preacher Pontiaan van Hattem, The Fall of the World's Idol. The city council confiscated and burned the first part in 1718, and Roggeveen moved to Flushing and then to Arnemuiden, where he published two further parts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 1 August 1721 Roggeveen, aged 62, set out at the head of an expedition paid for by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>, rival of the East Indies Company. His brother Jan helped plan it. The aims were to find Terra Australis and to open a western trade route to the Spice Islands. He had three ships, the Arend, the Thienhoven and the Afrikaansche Galey, with 223 men. He sailed to the Falkland Islands, which he renamed Belgia Australis, passed through the Strait of Le Maire and went beyond 60 degrees south before entering the Pacific. He landed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdivia">Valdivia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and spent from 24 February to 17 March 1722 at the Juan Fernandez Islands.</p><p>On Easter Sunday, 5 April 1722, the ships reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a>, where he reported seeing between 2,000 and 3,000 inhabitants. He then charted six islands in the Tuamotu Archipelago, two in the Society Islands and four in Samoa, and was the first European to find <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bora-bora">Bora Bora</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maupiti">Maupiti</a> and Samoa. He lost the Afrikaansche Galey at Takapoto atoll. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makatea">Makatea</a> he fired on a crowded beach after a violent encounter, and the islanders ambushed a shore party and killed ten of his crew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The two remaining ships passed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> in 1722. There the East Indies Company arrested Roggeveen for violating its monopoly and confiscated the ships. After a long lawsuit in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> the company was forced to compensate him for his losses and to pay his crew. After his return he published a fourth part of his religious work. He died on 31 January 1729.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacob Roggeveen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Recorded contact with Europeans began on Easter Sunday, 5 April 1722, when the Dutch navigator <b>Jacob Roggeveen</b> reached the island and gave it its European name.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rapa-nui">Rapa Nui · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>All of the moai erected on ahu were toppled in the years after <b>Jacob Roggeveen</b>'s arrival in 1722.</blockquote><a href="/work/moai">Moai · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch navigator <b>Jacob Roggeveen</b> became the first known European to reach Rapa Nui, naming it after the day of his arrival, Easter Sunday.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-5">5 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Middelburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in Middelburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.5,3.61&amp;year=1704" aria-label="Middelburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4632 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jacob Roggeveen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5082 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5082 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.5,3.61&amp;year=1704">What was here in 1704? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
